2186406784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186406776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406784 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4f78fc66-8357-5b97-ab1a-5d79ee0e7c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186406779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406785 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406786 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "077d2dca-c9d8-5043-92cb-18878e5ddc66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406786 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406787 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406787 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406787 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406787 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406787 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406787 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}})-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
